00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.290
Who can tell me what a proof is?

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.090
Any ideas of what is a proof anyway?

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:29.830
Any thoughts?

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:30.130
Yeah?

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.500
Very good.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.280
I like that.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.900
That's very close to what we're going to do here.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:45.660
Yeah?

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.880
Now that's a special kind of proof, though.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.340
That's a mathematical proof.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.660
And I'm going to write a definition very close to that

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:55.220
in a few minutes.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.000
But proofs exist beyond mathematics.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.520
Can anybody think of a higher level notion of what a

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.040
proof is?

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.420
That's correct what you said, but there's a higher metal

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.900
level notion of what a proof is.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.780
Beyond that, may have no logical deductions potentially.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.040
May have no assumptions.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:18.920
Any thoughts?

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:20.680
Got a proof?

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.480
Well, I think generally a proof is considered across

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.100
multiple fields as a method for

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.120
ascertaining the truth.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.060
And you described one method.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.780
Now by ascertaining, I mean establishing truth,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.920
verifying truth.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.960
And there's lots of ways to ascertain truth in society and

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:00.940
even within science.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.960
What are some examples of ways that we

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.460
ascertain truth in society?

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:10.320
Yeah?

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.170
Observation.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:17.850
Experiment and observation.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:18.390
Excellent.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.150
That's the bedrock of physics.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.460
I mean, who really knows if there's gravity out there?

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.590
Well, we observe it.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.590
And so we then conclude that's the truth.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:33.310
There's gravity and we have laws about it.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:34.330
That's one good way.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.490
What's another way of ascertaining truth across

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.070
scientific disciplines or in science, beyond

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:44.270
science just in society?

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:45.970
How is truth established?

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:48.330
What are the ways?

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.280
What things aren't true and that helps you narrow

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.000
down what is true?

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.040
Yes, truth?

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:57.760
Yeah, that's great.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:02:59.420
Truth is the opposite of falsehood.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.100
How do we establish falsehood?

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.680
What are the ways of doing that?

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.820
How do you decide something is not true?

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:07.740
In everyday life, yeah.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:10.980
Find counter examples.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:12.780
Yeah, that's good.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.980
So in fact, even a step more general sampling,

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.480
counter examples are ways.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.260
You know, if you do something and experiment 10 times

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.040
and every time it comes out one way,

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.300
that's truth, maybe.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.580
But there's fields where that becomes truth.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.160
What about other ways?

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.420
How are we going to decide if Roger Clemens

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.440
is guilty of perjury for lying about

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.360
steroids to Congress?

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.090
He did it or he didn't.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.190
How are we going to decide that?

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.090
What's the truth behind it?

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.590
How is that truth going to be ascertained?

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:56.410
Yeah?

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.730
Examining evidence and who makes the conclusion there?

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:04.570
The jury.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.590
Truth is established by juries or judges.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.290
You know, Blago, I can never pronounce his name.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.530
Illinois Governor, Blago Djavic, he's guilty.

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:21.510
That's the truth.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:22.770
Okay?

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.250
Of, you know, not of conspiracy,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.330
trying to sell Obama's Senate seat,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.970
but of lying to the authorities about campaign financing.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:34.450
O.J. is guilty, not of killing his wife,

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.750
but of breaking into an apartment

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.230
to steal back some of his merchandise.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:40.890
Okay?

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.950
So judges and juries make decisions on truth.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.690
What are other truths, bigger truths even

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.170
than judges and juries in society?

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.860
There's one really big one that causes a lot of issues, yeah?

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:55.300
Religion.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:56.160
What is it?

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.040
Religion, the word of God.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:00.860
Broadly construed here.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.360
Or religion.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:04.680
You know?

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.100
Now that one's really hard to argue about.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.260
You know, because you believe it.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:10.980
You know?

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.200
And especially if you're not talking to God regularly

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.000
and somebody is, well, it's hard to argue about,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.060
you know, the truth.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.000
So you rely on others to interpret it for you often.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.220
You know, a priest or minister or rabbi.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:23.620
Okay?

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.580
And it gets complicated because you can end up

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.420
with conflicting truths based on, you know,

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.780
who you think you're talking to

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.560
or who your translator is for you.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.640
You know, another one is the word of your boss.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.330
Whatever the boss says is right.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.010
Often in business, the customer is always right.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.110
That's the truth, whatever the customer says.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:45.850
All right?

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:48.110
You know, with Donald Trump as your boss,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.790
you better agree or you're fired, right?

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.400
Often in classes, the professor says it is true

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.840
because the authority said it.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:05:59.600
That's not true here.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:00.320
All right?

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:00.940
That will not hold.

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:04.160
And one of the nicest things about math that I like a lot

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.420
is that the youngest student can stand up against

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.440
the most, oldest, most experienced professor

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.880
and win an argument on mathematics.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.200
In fact, I really do get pleasure

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.720
when a student comes up and proves me wrong.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.960
I loved it when that student came in and she showed me

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.780
what I said really wasn't right

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.800
when you looked at it carefully or in a different light.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.580
You know, now, sometimes if I do it on the board here

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.520
and it's in class, well, you know, that's fun.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.360
I feel a little embarrassed afterwards,

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.980
but it's a good thing about mathematics

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.260
is that you can have that kind of dialogue.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:41.760
Okay.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:45.400
Another one, which is related to the word of God sometimes,

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.000
is inner conviction.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.700
Very popular in computer science, believe it or not.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:53.600
You know, with the mantra,

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.280
there are no bugs in my program.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.880
Can't tell you how many times, you know, you hear that.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.320
Closely related is I don't see why not.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:05.980
Something is true.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:10.180
And that's a good one because that transfers the burden

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.700
of proof to anybody who disagrees with you.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:15.060
You don't have to prove it to say,

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.180
I don't see why it's not true.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.300
All of a sudden, the other person who's questioning you

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.640
becomes their job to disprove you,

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.000
which is not so good.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:24.180
Okay.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.320
Now, in mathematics, there's this higher level

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.900
and someone stated it very clearly up there.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.440
Let me write it up here.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.460
In mathematics, we have a mathematical proof,

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:45.700
is a verification of a proposition

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:03.120
by a chain of logical deductions

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:16.510
from a set of axioms.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.420
Now, that's a bit of a mouthful.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.660
There's three important components here.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:33.140
Propositions, logical deductions, and axioms.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.300
And we're going to spend the rest of the class today

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:38.500
talking about each of these

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:39.760
and then give an example of a proof.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.540
We'll start with propositions.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:52.890
A proposition is a statement

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:03.930
that is either true or false.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.300
You may not know which one,

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:09.880
but it's one or the other.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.440
Simple example, 2 plus 3 equals 5.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:16.720
Now, that's a true proposition.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.100
Here's one that's a little more interesting.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:31.490
For all n in the set of natural numbers,

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:38.550
n squared plus n plus 41 is a prime number.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.510
Now, I've used some notation here.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:50.290
This is the upside down a is the for all symbol.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.060
How many people have not seen that symbol before?

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:57.020
I had a bunch of you.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:59.460
You're going to see a bunch of symbols here first week.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.860
And that means for every possible choice of n,

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.100
and this is in the natural numbers,

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:11.280
which is the set, 0, 1, 2, 3, and so forth.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:12.920
It's the natural numbers.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:15.520
It's basically the integers, but not negative.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.690
So we're saying for every natural number,

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.490
i.e. for 0, for 1, for 2, for 3, and so forth,

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.310
this expression is a prime.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:31.890
Now, a prime number is a number that is not divisible

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.090
by any other number besides itself in one.

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:40.510
So 2, 3, 5, 7, or prime, 9 is not because it's 3 times 3.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:45.630
Now, this part here is called the predicate.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:11:00.810
And a predicate is a proposition whose truth depends

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:10.970
on the value of a variable, in this case n.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:20.500
This is referred to as the universe of discourse.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.740
It's the space of all things we're talking about.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:26.720
We're only talking about natural numbers here.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.560
This is called a quantifier.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.640
We'll see more quantifiers later.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:38.530
All right, now to see if this proposition is true,

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.830
we need to make sure that this predicate

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.230
is true for every natural number n.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:49.250
So let's see if we can check that.

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.010
Let's try some values.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:57.650
So we'll try n is 1, 2, 3, and so forth.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:01.390
And we'll compute n squared plus n plus 41.

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:03.490
And then we'll check, is it prime?

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.920
So for n equals 0, n squared plus n plus 41 is 41.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.260
Is 41 prime?

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:19.250
Yeah, nothing divides 41 but itself in one.

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:22.720
All right, let's try 1.

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.180
1 squared plus 1 plus 41 is 43.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:26.860
43 prime.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:28.380
Yes.

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:31.280
Let's try 2.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.100
We've got 4 plus 2 is 6 plus 41.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:35.680
47.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.620
Is 47 prime?

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:38.460
Yes.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:39.240
Yes.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:40.960
Looking good.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:45.420
3, I got 9, 12, 53.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:47.260
53 prime?

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:48.820
Yeah.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:51.640
And I could keep on going here.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:52.800
I could go down to 20.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.820
I get 420, 461.

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:58.720
In fact, that is a prime.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.520
And I could just keep on going here.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:03.220
Go down to 39.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.080
I get 1,601.

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:08.220
You can check that as a prime.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:13.040
The first 40 values of n, the proposition is true.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:13.820
The predicate is true.

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:15.260
It is prime.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:20.200
Now, this is a great example because in a lot of fields,

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:22.640
physics, for example, statistics often,

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:26.580
if it's true, you checked 40 examples.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:28.260
That's above and beyond the call of duty.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:29.780
It's always true.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:32.660
So yeah, this must be true, right?

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:35.530
No.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:36.770
Wrong.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.370
Often you'll see this in a lot of scientific fields.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:41.570
It is not true.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.090
Can anybody give me an example of n

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:46.410
for which n squared plus n plus 41 is not prime?

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:46.850
Yeah.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:47.510
40.

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:48.830
40.

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:49.510
Good.

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:50.650
Let's see about 40.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:57.850
40 squared plus 40 plus 41 is 1681.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:00.430
What's that equal?

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:03.680
41 squared.

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:06.980
So it is not prime.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:10.400
Somebody give me an obvious example where it's not prime.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:11.800
41.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:13.180
Yeah, 41 squared.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:14.880
Everything is divided by 41.

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:16.940
But 40 is the first break point.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:21.360
So the first 40 examples work and then it fails.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:24.420
So this proposition is false,

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:27.740
even though it was looking pretty good.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:30.040
There's a reason I'm doing this effect.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:31.080
I'm going to do it some more here.

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.140
I'm going to beat you over the head with it.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:39.200
Here's a famous in mathematics statement.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:44.220
A to the fourth plus B to the fourth

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:48.600
plus C to the fourth equals D to the fourth

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:57.360
has no positive integer solutions.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:02.010
That is a proposition.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:05.810
Now, this proposition was conjectured to be true

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.470
by Euler in 1769.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.010
Euler's a big honcho in math.

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:12.810
We still talk about him a lot,

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:14.790
even though he's been dead for centuries.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:19.070
It was unsolved for over two centuries.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:21.170
Mathematicians worked on it.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:25.130
It was finally disproved by a very clever fellow

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:26.170
named Noam Elkes.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.770
218 years later, after it was conjectured,

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.790
he works at that other school down the street.

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.230
And he came up with this.

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:39.990
A equals 95,800.

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:45.210
B equals 217,519.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:49.810
C equals 414,560.

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:51.490
You don't have to remember these numbers.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:53.550
We're not going to quiz you on that.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:55.910
422,481.

00:15:57.150 --> 00:15:59.310
Now, he claims I've never personally checked it,

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:00.150
but presumably people have.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:03.190
You plug those in here and you have an equality.

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:04.910
So he says.

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:08.550
So in fact, the correct true proposition is

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:13.090
there do exist A, B, C, D

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:16.450
in the positive natural numbers

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:19.510
such that A to the fourth plus B to the fourth

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:21.910
plus C to the fourth equals D to the fourth.

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:24.970
All right, I used a new quantifier here

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:27.210
called there exists.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.510
Instead of an upside down A, it's a backwards E.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:31.670
Don't ask me why.

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:33.650
That's what it is.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:38.230
The plus means you can't have

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:39.710
zero or negative numbers.

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:42.010
So these are the positive natural numbers.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:46.650
And here's your predicate,

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:51.290
which of course the truth of this depends

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:52.870
on the values of A, B, C, and D.

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:56.540
All right, it took a long time to figure out

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:58.280
that actually there was a solution here.

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:00.720
Obviously everything they tried to let time failed.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:02.100
Let me give you another one.

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:12.610
313 x cubed plus y cubed

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.110
equals z cubed

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:24.650
has no positive integer solutions.

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:30.640
This turns out to be false,

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:34.220
but the shortest, smallest counter example

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.180
has over a thousand digits.

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:38.580
This one was easy.

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:39.500
They only have six digits.

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:42.820
So there's no way ever you'd use a computer

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:46.220
to exhaustively search a thousand digit numbers here

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:47.240
to show it's false.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:50.700
Now of course some of you are probably thinking

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:54.600
why on earth would I care

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:59.280
if 313 x cubed plus y cubed equals z cubed

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:01.220
has a solution?

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.080
Now that probably won't be the last time

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:05.960
that thought occurs to you during the term.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:08.840
And why on earth would anybody

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:11.300
ever try to even find a solution to that?

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.880
You know, I mean mathematicians are sort of a rare breed.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.160
Now actually in this case

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.580
that's really important in practice.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.920
This equation is an example

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:25.320
of what's called an elliptic curve.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:26.960
Elliptic curve and you study these

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:29.540
if you're really a specialist in mathematics

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:30.560
in graduate school

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:33.680
or if you work for certain three letter agencies

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.680
because it's central to the understanding

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:38.280
of how to factor large integers.

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:41.300
That means factoring,

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:43.520
showing that what was it?

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.000
1681 is 41 x 41.

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:49.020
And I said okay, who cares about factoring?

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:52.520
Well factoring is the way

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.440
to break crypto systems like RSA

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:58.560
which are used for everything

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:00.280
that we do electronically today.

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.000
You have a PayPal account,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.140
you buy something online, you're using SSL.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:07.120
They're all using crypto systems

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.520
most of which are almost all

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:09.680
which are based on number theory.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.280
And in particular, they're based on factoring.

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:14.440
And if you can find good solutions

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:15.680
to things like this

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.060
or solutions to things like this

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:19.600
all of a sudden you can get

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.220
an angle and a wedge on factoring.

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:24.220
And it's because of that

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:26.720
that now RSA uses

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:27.900
thousand digit

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.240
modelluses instead of

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.620
hundred digit modelluses like they used to use

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:34.540
because people figured out how to factor them

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:35.940
and how to break the crypto system.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.340
If you can break those crypto systems

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.420
you can't rule the world

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:40.920
but it's close.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.520
So you'll learn, we'll talk more about this

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:46.660
the week after next when we do number theory

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:48.520
and we work up to RSA

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:49.880
and how that crypto system works

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:51.300
and why factoring is so important.

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:54.600
So you all don't have to

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:56.220
really have to worry about this

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:57.380
but these things are important.

00:19:57.680 --> 00:19:59.740
And the bigger message is

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.600
that you don't just try a few cases

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:03.320
and if it works you think it's done.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:04.960
That's not

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:06.820
how the game works in mathematics.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:08.500
You can get an idea of maybe it's true

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:11.000
but it doesn't tell you the answer.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:14.340
All right, let me give it another one.

00:20:15.140 --> 00:20:16.820
This is another very famous one

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.200
that probably most of you have heard of.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:20.380
The regions

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:23.740
in any map

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.180
can be colored

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:32.390
in four colors

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:35.500
so that

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:38.300
adjacent regions

00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:46.540
have different colors.

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:48.960
Like a map of the United States

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:50.240
every state gets a color

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:52.200
two states share a border

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:53.740
they have different colors

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:56.000
as you can distinguish them.

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:59.020
This is known as the four color theorem

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:00.740
and it's very famous

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:01.660
in the popular literature.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.220
How many people have heard of this theorem before?

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:06.580
Yeah, okay, so you've all heard of it.

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:08.080
As a long history

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:10.700
it was conjectured by somebody named Guthrie

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:11.560
in 1853.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:14.080
He's the first person to say this ought to be possible.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:15.740
And there were many

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:17.620
false proofs

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.860
over the ensuing century.

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:21.140
One of the most

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:23.240
convincing was a proof using pictures

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:26.040
by Kempe in 1879.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:27.840
You know, 26 years later.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:29.460
And this proof was

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:30.860
believed for over a decade.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:32.900
Mathematicians thought the proof was right

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:35.740
until another mathematician named He-Wood

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:37.340
found a fatal flaw

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:38.100
in the argument.

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:39.900
Now this proof

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.340
by Kempe consisted of drawing pictures

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:44.260
of what maps have to look like.

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:46.020
You start by saying a map

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:47.680
has to look like one of these types.

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:49.040
And he would draw pictures of them.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.840
And then he argued that those types

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:53.480
that he drew pictures of it worked for it.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:56.460
Proofs by picture

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:58.700
are often very convincing

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:00.280
and very wrong.

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:02.040
And I'm going to give you one

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:03.340
to start lecture next time.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:05.480
Be proof by PowerPoint, which is even worse

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:06.260
than proof by picture.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:09.060
And it is compelling.

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:10.320
You know.

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:13.060
The point will be to show you proofs by picture

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:14.880
are generally not a good thing.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:16.780
Because your brain just locks in.

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:17.900
That's what it has to look like.

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:20.520
And you don't think about other ways

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:21.680
that it might look like.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:24.600
Now the four color theorem was finally proved

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:26.740
by Oppel and Hocken

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:27.940
in 1977.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:30.360
But they had to use a computer

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.640
to check thousands of cases.

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:35.020
Now

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:36.480
this was a little disturbing

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:37.760
to mathematicians because

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:40.780
how do they know the computer did the right thing?

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:42.640
You know

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:44.660
your colleague writes a proof on the board.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:46.000
You can check it. But how do you know

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.100
the computer didn't mess up or not do some cases?

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:50.280
Now

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:51.800
everybody believes it's true now.

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:54.020
But it's unsatisfying.

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:55.600
A few years ago

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.480
a 12 page human proof was discovered.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:01.300
But it's not been verified.

00:23:01.300 --> 00:23:03.640
And people are very suspicious of it

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.560
because the proof of the main lemma

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:06.980
says, quote

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:09.360
details of this lemma

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.520
is left to the reader, C figure 7.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:13.280
That's what the main lemma

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:14.800
of the proof is. But people think

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:16.180
there maybe were some good ideas there.

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:17.660
But very suspicious proof.

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:19.380
Alright.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:22.200
Let's do another one.

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:23.440
Another proposition.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:25.340
Also very famous.

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:28.820
Every even integer

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:33.270
but 2

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:36.090
actually positive integer

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:36.570
but 2

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:38.470
is the sum

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:42.010
of 2 primes.

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:46.120
For example

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:51.540
24 is the sum of 11 and 13

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:52.900
which are prime.

00:23:56.960 --> 00:23:58.540
Anybody know is this true or false?

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:00.460
This proposition.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:04.180
Yeah?

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.040
Yeah, that's right. Me too.

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:08.880
Nobody knows

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:10.460
if this is true or false.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:11.700
This is called

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:13.200
Golbach's conjecture.

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:15.860
It was conjectured by

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:18.200
Christian Golbach in 1742.

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:20.280
This is a really

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:21.320
simple proposition.

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:24.040
And it's amazing

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:25.500
it's not known. In fact I spent

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:28.280
a couple of years working on

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:30.000
I thought this has to be easy enough to prove

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:30.980
when I was younger.

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:33.460
And didn't get very far.

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:36.400
So people still don't know if it's true.

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:38.400
And in fact it was listed

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:39.080
by the globe

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:41.940
as one of the great unsolved mysteries.

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:43.980
So if you get out this globe article here

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.420
on the handout. Does everybody have this handout?

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:48.680
If you don't

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.340
we'll get it passed out. Somebody missing that handout

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:51.700
up over there and over here.

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:54.340
If we get those passed out.

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:58.360
Now it lists the three conjectures.

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:00.980
Do you see Golbach's conjecture there?

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:04.240
Now can anybody

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.060
point out something

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:07.500
that's a little disturbing about

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:09.920
what the globe says about

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:10.820
Golbach's conjecture?

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:16.710
Yeah. It gives the example

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:18.990
instead of 24 is 11 plus 13

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:19.810
it says 20

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:22.210
is the sum of 9 and 11.

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:24.590
Now

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:26.770
if we're allowed to use things like

00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:28.550
9 as primes, Golbach's conjecture

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:29.870
is pretty easy to prove. It's true.

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:34.530
This won't be the last time

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:36.170
we get examples from the literature.

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:37.230
In fact we're going to do this a lot.

00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:39.710
Along this theme of you cannot believe

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:40.730
everything you read.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:43.170
Now the globe is easy pickings but we'll do

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:44.130
some more interesting ones later.

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:47.630
Now this article lists two other famous conjectures

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.450
which most people believe to be true.

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:53.550
The Riemann Hypothesis

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.270
after a 1859 paper

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.650
written by Bernard Riemann

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:01.070
suggests that zeros

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:03.470
in an infinite series of numbers known as a zeta function

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:05.890
form along a straight line in the complex plane.

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:07.910
The hypothesis has been proved

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:09.770
to 1.5 billion zeros.

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:11.890
Not far enough to prove it

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:12.270
completely.

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:15.510
If they did 1.5 trillion zeros

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:17.590
it wouldn't be far enough to prove it completely, of course.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:19.530
And then

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:22.150
the

00:26:22.150 --> 00:26:24.350
the Riemann Hypothesis

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:25.230
a couple of years ago

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:27.690
somebody credible claimed to have proved it

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:29.330
proved turned out not to be right.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:32.010
Then there's the Poincare conjecture.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:34.050
Now this one was

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:35.170
finished off.

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:37.390
It was proved to be true in 2003

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:40.170
by a Russian named Grigor Perlman.

00:26:41.030 --> 00:26:42.250
The conjecture says

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:43.570
roughly speaking that

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:45.630
3D objects without

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:47.070
holes, like not a donut

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:48.830
are equivalent

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:51.210
to the sphere. They can sort of be deformed

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:52.290
into a sphere.

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:55.030
This is known to be true in four dimensions

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:57.150
and higher but nobody could prove it for three dimensions

00:26:57.150 --> 00:26:58.350
until Perlman came along.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:01.650
Now there's a bit of a controversy around this guy.

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:03.990
He had an 80 page proof

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:05.150
but didn't have all the details.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:08.370
So then other teams of mathematicians got together

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:10.070
and wrote 350 pages

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:11.330
of details

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:14.010
and then most people believe now

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:16.270
that it's right and that his original proof

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:17.690
might not have had all the details

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:19.710
but he had the right structure of the proof.

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:22.250
So he won prizes

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:23.950
for this. He won the highest prize

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:25.250
in mathematics, the Fields Medal

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:27.810
and just earlier this year

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:29.870
he was awarded the 1 million dollar

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:31.910
Millennium Prize and there's about

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:34.010
six problems or so that if you solve one

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:35.990
of them the Clay Institute gives you a million

00:27:35.990 --> 00:27:37.490
dollars and he's the first one

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:39.010
to win the million dollars.

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:40.970
Now the guy's a little strange.

00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:43.590
He rejected the Fields Medal and refused

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:45.870
to go to the ceremony where he was being honored

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:48.430
and he's recently rejected the Millennium Prize.

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:51.330
In any way

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:53.910
this area is murky and we have an expert

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:55.990
to explain it all

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:57.750
for us on video which I thought I'd show.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:07.360
Alright let's do

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:09.320
a simpler one here.

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:14.320
For all n

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:15.240
in z

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:18.600
n greater than or equal to 2

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:20.040
implies

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:23.240
n squared greater than or equal to 4.

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:24.660
Now z

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.040
we use for the integers

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.350
and so that would be

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:33.870
0, 1, minus 1, 2,

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:36.130
minus 2 and so forth

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:38.070
and this symbol

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:39.950
here is

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:40.630
implies.

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:45.960
Alright in fact

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:47.940
one thing you can notice when you read the text

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:50.320
is we use different notation

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:52.200
there as the standard

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:53.300
that I will use in lecture

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:55.900
and there's lots of ways of doing it.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:57.920
You could have a double arrow, a single arrow

00:28:57.920 --> 00:28:59.880
you could write out implies every time

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:01.740
as done in the text and it doesn't really

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:03.160
matter which one you want to use

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.180
as long as you use one of the conventions

00:29:05.180 --> 00:29:05.960
for implies.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:08.600
And let me define what

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:10.460
implies means.

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:27.690
An implication

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:35.470
p implies q

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:37.510
is

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:38.610
said to be true

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:40.630
if

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:42.610
p is false

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:44.890
or q

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:45.910
is true

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:47.770
either one.

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:51.030
So we can write this down in terms of a truth table

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:56.000
as follows

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.440
we have the values of p and q

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:01.380
and I'll give the value of p implies q

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:03.900
if p is q

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:05.120
and q is true

00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:06.860
sorry p is true and q is true

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:08.800
what about p implies q?

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:13.860
It's true because q is true

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:14.880
the definition

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:18.100
if p is true and q is false

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:20.480
false

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:23.640
p is false

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:24.480
q is true

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:30.360
what about false and false

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:32.980
is true

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:34.640
even though

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:36.600
this is false as long as p is false

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:38.420
p implies q is true

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:42.660
so this is important to remember

00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:45.100
false implies anything

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:47.180
is true

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:48.840
which is a little

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.740
strange there's a famous expression

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:52.840
if pigs could fly

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:53.740
I would be king

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:56.400
is that true?

00:31:00.080 --> 00:31:02.580
In fact this statement

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:05.440
pigs fly

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:08.130
implies

00:31:09.390 --> 00:31:10.230
I'm king

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:13.880
that's true

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:17.240
because pigs don't fly

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:19.740
it doesn't matter whether or not I'm king

00:31:19.740 --> 00:31:20.760
which I'm not

00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:22.320
since pigs don't fly

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:23.760
even though that's false

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:26.120
the implication is true

00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:29.200
now some of you have worked on these things before

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:31.200
second nature if you haven't

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:32.620
want to start getting familiar

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:34.060
with that

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:37.520
alright let's do another example

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:50.690
what about this proposition

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:54.390
for all n for all integers

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:55.170
n and z

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:58.350
n greater than or equal to 2

00:31:58.350 --> 00:32:01.880
this is if and only if

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:05.960
n squared greater than or equal to 4

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:08.340
is that true

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:15.530
is n bigger than 2 if and only

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:16.570
if n squared is bigger than 4

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:18.770
it's false

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:20.870
what's an example of n for which that's false

00:32:22.250 --> 00:32:22.810
negative

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:24.470
alright so it's false

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:26.790
n equals negative

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:27.310
3

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:29.150
alright

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:32.510
negative 3 squared is bigger than or equal to 4

00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:34.390
but negative 3 is not bigger than or equal to 2

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:34.990
and in fact

00:32:35.810 --> 00:32:37.430
this if and only if means

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:40.770
you have to have an implication

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:41.450
both ways

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:44.950
so you got to check both ways

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:46.750
for it so let's do the truth table

00:32:46.750 --> 00:32:48.170
extend this truth table out here

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:52.710
to do the truth table for p

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:55.070
if and only if q

00:32:55.070 --> 00:33:05.030
alright so here

00:33:05.030 --> 00:33:05.830
p and q

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:09.570
is q implies p

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:10.930
true for this row

00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:13.590
does q imply

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:14.490
true imply true

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:16.850
yeah

00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:19.150
false implies true

00:33:19.150 --> 00:33:21.620
that's true

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:24.040
true does not imply false that's false

00:33:24.040 --> 00:33:26.180
and false implies false

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:28.740
and so now we can see

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:29.940
where p

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.800
is if and only if q

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:34.160
if they're both

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:35.380
true

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:36.640
then it's true here

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:40.400
what about here is p true if and only if q is true

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:41.420
in this case

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:44.560
no

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:47.060
because p implies q is false

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:48.960
but q implies p is true

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:49.640
so it's false

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:51.600
false here

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:55.860
I made a mistake there right

00:33:55.860 --> 00:33:56.960
that was true

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:00.380
and true

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:01.260
if and only if true

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:03.760
they're both true so we're okay

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:06.420
so p if and only if q

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:07.800
is true when they're both true

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:08.920
or both false

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:10.220
that's it

00:34:10.220 --> 00:34:12.460
they're different then it's not true

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:15.700
the key here is to always check

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:16.800
both ways

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:19.620
so if you're asked to prove an if and only if

00:34:19.620 --> 00:34:21.480
you got to prove that way

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:22.700
and that way

00:34:25.710 --> 00:34:28.710
alright we've just done about 15 propositions

00:34:29.850 --> 00:34:31.130
is every sentence

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:31.730
a proposition?

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:36.700
yes? no?

00:34:37.680 --> 00:34:39.960
no what's an example of something that's not a proposition

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:43.210
a what?

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:44.810
this statement is false

00:34:44.810 --> 00:34:46.630
this statement is false

00:34:47.450 --> 00:34:48.130
that's true

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:51.070
it's not a proposition

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:53.010
because if it were true

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.890
it wouldn't be false and if it was false

00:34:54.890 --> 00:34:56.090
then it would be true and you have a contradiction

00:34:56.510 --> 00:34:58.330
so it's neither true nor false

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:01.330
what's a more simple example of something that's not a proposition

00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:08.480
boy I would have said that's true

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:10.380
in some some world

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.500
you know because that yeah that's a tissue

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.220
so it's a true statement

00:35:17.420 --> 00:35:17.940
hello

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:20.100
that's good that's neither true nor false

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:22.100
yeah a question

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:23.140
who are you?

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:25.620
neither true nor false so not everything

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:28.720
is a proposition but this course pretty much everything we deal with

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:30.140
will be a proposition

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:32.260
alright so

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:34.160
that's it for propositions

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:35.520
any questions on propositions?

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:39.620
alright next we're going to talk about

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:41.440
axioms

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:42.600
now

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.540
the good news is that axioms are the same thing

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:51.640
really is propositions

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:54.000
the only difference is that

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:55.940
axioms are propositions

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:57.380
that we just assume are true

00:36:01.720 --> 00:36:02.580
an axiom

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:05.020
is a proposition

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:11.720
that is assumed

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:16.260
to be true

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:20.890
there's no proof

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:22.350
that an axiom is true

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:24.310
you just assume it

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:25.530
because you think it's reasonable

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:28.470
in fact the word axiom comes from

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:30.190
greek it doesn't mean

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:31.910
to be true

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:33.450
it means to think worthy

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.690
something you think is worthy enough to be assumed

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:37.190
to be true

00:36:37.190 --> 00:36:39.750
now a lot of times you hear people say

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:41.650
sometimes we'll even say it to you

00:36:41.650 --> 00:36:44.650
you know don't make assumptions when you're doing math

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:46.230
no

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:47.110
that's not true

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:49.470
you have to make assumptions when you do math

00:36:49.470 --> 00:36:50.790
otherwise you can't do anything

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:52.770
because you got to start with some axioms

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:54.670
the key in math

00:36:54.670 --> 00:36:55.730
is to identify

00:36:56.630 --> 00:36:59.210
what your assumptions are so people can see them

00:37:00.010 --> 00:37:01.370
and the idea is

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:02.410
that when you do a proof

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.890
anybody who agrees with your assumptions

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:05.850
or your axioms

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:09.070
can follow your proof and they have to agree with your conclusion

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:10.830
now they might disagree

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:13.710
with your axioms in which case they're not going to buy your

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:14.530
proof

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.850
now there are lots of axioms used in math

00:37:17.850 --> 00:37:19.550
for example

00:37:21.630 --> 00:37:23.730
if a equals

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:24.210
b

00:37:24.210 --> 00:37:26.870
and b equals c

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:28.250
then

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:30.130
a equals c

00:37:30.970 --> 00:37:32.850
there is no proof of that

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:35.410
but it seems pretty good

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:38.110
and so we just throw it in the bucket of

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:39.450
axioms and use it

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:41.740
now

00:37:43.120 --> 00:37:45.420
axioms can be contradictory in different

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:46.300
context

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:48.280
here's a good example

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:50.660
in Euclidean geometry

00:37:53.890 --> 00:37:55.900
there's a central axiom

00:37:56.030 --> 00:37:56.630
that says

00:37:57.510 --> 00:37:58.110
given

00:37:59.210 --> 00:38:00.610
a line

00:38:01.590 --> 00:38:02.530
l

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:04.350
and a point

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:06.150
p

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.550
not on l

00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:11.700
there is

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:18.810
exactly one line

00:38:19.910 --> 00:38:21.530
through p

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:22.310
parallel to l

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:29.970
you all saw this in geometry

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:31.390
in middle school right

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:33.930
at a point in a line there's exactly one line

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:36.030
another line through the point that's parallel to the line

00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:39.380
now there's also a field

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:40.740
called spherical geometry

00:38:44.430 --> 00:38:46.050
and there you have an axiom

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:47.670
that contradicts this

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:50.410
it says given a line l and a point p

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:51.190
not on l

00:38:52.010 --> 00:38:53.730
there is no

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:56.050
line

00:38:56.050 --> 00:38:58.110
through p parallel to l

00:38:58.850 --> 00:38:59.750
on the sphere

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:02.850
there's a field called

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:04.690
hyperbolic

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:06.710
geometry

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:09.660
and there

00:39:09.660 --> 00:39:11.920
there's an axiom that says given a line l

00:39:11.920 --> 00:39:13.100
and a point p not on l

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:14.600
there are

00:39:15.940 --> 00:39:16.860
infinitely many

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:25.020
lines through p parallel to l

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:27.500
so how can this be

00:39:27.500 --> 00:39:29.720
does that mean one of these fields is totally bogus

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:30.480
or two of them are

00:39:31.240 --> 00:39:33.300
because they've got contradictory axioms

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:34.900
that's okay

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:37.020
just whatever field you're in

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:38.040
state your axioms

00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:40.100
and they do make sense

00:39:40.100 --> 00:39:41.480
in their various fields

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:43.660
you know this is planar geometry

00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:45.200
this is on the sphere and this is on

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:47.300
hyperbolic geometry

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:49.040
they make sense in those contexts

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:51.280
so you can have

00:39:51.280 --> 00:39:54.120
more or less whatever axioms you want

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:56.440
there are sort of two guiding principles

00:39:56.440 --> 00:39:57.160
to axioms

00:40:15.370 --> 00:40:16.510
axioms should be

00:40:16.510 --> 00:40:18.490
it's called consistent

00:40:21.380 --> 00:40:22.420
and complete

00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:23.440
now

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:29.420
a set of axioms

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:34.840
is consistent

00:40:39.790 --> 00:40:41.130
if no proposition

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:47.380
can be proved to be both

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:48.080
true and false

00:40:48.080 --> 00:40:56.520
and you can see why that's important

00:40:56.520 --> 00:40:59.300
you know if you spend three weeks

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:01.020
proving something's true

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:03.660
and the next day somebody proves it's also

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:04.360
false

00:41:04.360 --> 00:41:06.020
I mean the whole thing was pointless

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:09.140
so it only makes sense if your axioms as a group

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:10.200
are consistent

00:41:10.200 --> 00:41:12.940
a set of axioms

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:14.060
is said to be complete

00:41:25.710 --> 00:41:28.030
if it can be used

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:32.710
to prove

00:41:34.150 --> 00:41:35.270
every proposition

00:41:35.270 --> 00:41:36.850
is either true or false

00:41:36.850 --> 00:41:37.690
alright

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:50.480
now this is desirable because it means

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:52.920
well you can solve every problem

00:41:53.800 --> 00:41:54.900
alright everything is

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:56.520
you can prove it's true

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:57.340
or you can prove it's false

00:41:57.340 --> 00:41:59.300
you can get to the end

00:41:59.300 --> 00:42:05.600
now you'd think it shouldn't be too hard

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:07.540
to get a set of axioms that satisfies

00:42:07.540 --> 00:42:08.940
these two basic properties

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:11.380
you're allowed to choose whatever you want

00:42:11.380 --> 00:42:13.340
really just you don't want to

00:42:13.340 --> 00:42:14.600
be creating contradictions

00:42:14.600 --> 00:42:16.760
and you want a set that's powerful enough

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:18.800
that allows you to prove everything is true or false

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:19.440
one of the two

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:22.560
turned out not to be so easy to do this

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:23.760
in fact

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:26.840
many logicians spent their careers

00:42:27.580 --> 00:42:29.340
famous logicians trying to

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:30.480
find a set of axioms

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:32.220
just one set that was

00:42:32.220 --> 00:42:34.160
consistent and complete

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:36.240
in fact Russell and Whitehead are probably

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:37.080
the two most famous

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:39.960
they spent their entire careers doing this

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:41.200
and they never got there

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:43.980
then one day this guy named Kurt Gertl

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:44.460
showed up

00:42:45.260 --> 00:42:46.960
and in the 1930s

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:49.300
he proved it's not possible

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.700
that there exist any set of axioms

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:53.600
that are both consistent

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:54.840
and complete

00:42:56.000 --> 00:42:57.980
now this discovery devastated

00:42:57.980 --> 00:42:59.100
the field

00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:00.860
it was a huge discovery

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:02.500
you know imagine

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:03.940
poor Russell and Whitehead

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:05.900
they spent their entire careers

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:07.660
going after this holy grail

00:43:07.660 --> 00:43:10.320
then Kurt shows up and said hey guys there's no grail

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:11.340
doesn't exist

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.300
and that's a little depressing

